A||||12||Gold-86SE||Iron Duke Problems...||||9||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||04-18-2000||10:17 AM||||Guys been reading the forum for a while now, have taken the information and used it in working on my '84 SE, but I am still at a loss as to why it runs the way it does.<P>First a little history.<BR>Purchased the Fiero about 3 months ago, owner told me it had been sitting around 1 year.<P>Here is what I have done since the purchase, replaced the distributor cap and rotor, plugs, wires, tbi injector, and fuel filter.<P>Here is my problem, once I start the engine it will not run unless I hold down the gas pedal it runs around 900 rpm, even then it runs like C.R.A.P.<P>I switched out the ignition coil from another  '84, thinking I was not getting enough fire. I also checked fuel pump at it seems to be fine.<P>Does anyone have any idea what the problem is?||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||04-18-2000||12:33 PM||||check for vacuum leaks. (Lot o threads on that.....)<P>check static(base) timing. static timing is used by the PIM untill 6-800RPM when the ECM takes timing control over.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-18-2000||02:15 PM||||Asdie from the above message, Also check Catatlytic converter if it is starting to clog up it will build up excess backpressure and choke engine a little. If cat is clogging up it may also seem to run very rich. It could also have a blown head gasket. Check to see if there is water in the oil or oil in the antifreeze. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||04-18-2000||02:23 PM||||You didn't mention how many miles it had or why it was sitting for so long. Could be a multitude of things. Among other things you can check is the EGR valve. You can get bad idle from a stick EGR vavle. If its been sitting it could be bad. It sounds like you have almost changed all the external things that would give you problems. You might soon have to to investigate internal things. Sticky valve, or a lifter that isn't pumped up and its always a good idea to check your compression to get a good idea of whats going on. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||04-18-2000||04:06 PM||||ogre - i'll check for vacuum leaks and i'll check the timing.<P>oreif - you said to check the cat. I actually pulled off the exhaust system (except for a straight pipe to send the exhaust out under the car) and the removal made no difference in the engine performance.<P>oreif - as for the head gasket, i am concerned that it has a blown gasket because when i changed the oil it looked brown and thick (not the usual look of black dirty oil) and i have witnessed white smoke from the tail pipe. But for this problem that I am having, is a head gasket really the problem?<P>WKDFIRO - the mileage is 87590. Here is what I remember about it's sitting for a while.  The owner was a honda dealer, his brother bought the car with high hopes, the honda dealer sold it to a girl who brought it back (she and her boyfriend broke up - the dealer was gracious being that she was a repeat customer).  During the time that the honda dealer had the fiero the clutch was replaced, then it sat for a while. I dont know much else.  Additionally, I have not yet checked the compression.<P>Additional info about items fixed/replaced, while I was working on the Fiero I received a map sensor error code, and put on a new map sensor.<P>This fiero is intended to be my wife's, and she wants me to add that there is a  galoping of the engine and the bumpy feeling while it runs at idle.  Bear in mind that the idle is about 900 rpm while holding the gas pedal down on the floor.<P>btw, read in a thread that there is a tach filter, is this true for the '84 Duke 4spd and where is it located?  If so how do i disconnect it?||||reg||7
Z||000005||Patrick||04-18-2000||04:55 PM||||<BR>Oh, oh...<P>Thick, <I>brown</I> oil. Bad sign. Definitely sounds like water in the crankcase.<P>A blown head gasket could certainly cause you all kinds of problems. Lack of compression on one or more cylinders because of a blown gasket could result in the symptoms you unfortunately have.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Motorhead||04-18-2000||05:26 PM||||While you've got the head off you might want to have it die checked. I found three small cracks in mine as a result of this simple operation.  Mine had been sitting for about a year, and had been severly overheated shortly before.  I elected to rebuild. She now PURRRSSS!||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||04-18-2000||07:19 PM||||Duke heads are NOTORIOUS for cracking. Time for a compression to confirm the worst!||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||04-18-2000||08:34 PM||||Yup. Compression test time.<P>You also need to verify completion of the recall work. Look for a lable in the engine compartment. If the lable is not there then call pontiac and/or area dealers and run the VIN. DO NOT do any more work untill the recall is verified!<P>The recall for 84 adds a number of shields to the engine and a number of other checks/repairs. The final version of the recall paperwork is 88-C-23 dated 06/90. "This campaign bulletin suppliments bulletin 84-C-15B [03/89] and supersedes bulletin 87-C-11 [01/89]. please discard 87-C-11."<P>84's are known for failing the front side head bolts. replacement of these bolts was part of the recall for 84-86 4 cyl. If this was not done then one or more of those bolts may have failed. (stretched or broken)<P>If you remove the head DO NOT use the original bolts. Replace the whole bolt set as well as all gaskets involved. DO NOT eliminate the large aluminum shield under the exhaust manifold. (Install order... shield, gasket, then manifold. Yes, only one gasket.)||||reg||1
Z||000009||Gold-86SE||04-19-2000||12:16 AM||||Update for the Iron Duke<P>First, thanks for the ideas, my wife and I checked several areas.<P>Ogre - I checked the timing on the Fiero, it seems to have been severly advanced. I was not able to determine the exact timing mark, but based on what I saw, the 8 degree indication on the deck lid, and the performance of the engine things are looking up.  It runs 10x better than when I bought it.<P>As for checking the recall work, my wife called 1-800-PM-Cares and the local dealer, seems that all recalls have been done.  And there is a metal sticker located in the engine bay and inside the front deck lid indicating the warning of proper maintenance.<P><BR>Here is what I am currently receiving from the ECM:<P>33 - MAP<BR>(I think I have a vacuum leak, since the MAP is new), and<P>45 - Rich Exhaust<BR>(probably needs an 02 sensor - why not replace it, seems like I have replaced every other sensor).<P><BR>Any ideas on how to verify my timing and ideas about the 33?<P>Gold-86SE<p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 04-19-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000010||Patrick||04-19-2000||12:46 AM||||<BR>You could change every sensor and also get the timing perfect, but still be facing a huge problem if the head gasket is blown. <P>We all want you to do a compression check. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Haze_Performance||04-19-2000||02:50 AM||||Hey guys, what should the compression be for the 4 banger?? ||||reg||5
Z||000012||theogre||04-19-2000||09:19 AM||||If I remember right, can't look it up right now, 110-120PSI on a 9:1 motor is about right.(Below 100-105 is rebuild time on the 4 cyl I think.) <P>Main thing is to make sure they are close to the same compression even if they are low. The range from highest to lowest must not be more than 10PSI, and preferably less. More than 10PSI and you have a definate problem.<P>If the head is removed for any reason on a 4 cyl the head bolts must be replaced EVEN IF the recall was done. The recall doesn't require the set be replaced, only the front ones. That leaves at least 4 old ones if all the front ones got replaced.((Odds are very good that none of the head bolts were replaced. It is very common that dealers don't fully comply with recall orders. There is no oversight of recall work by either the car makers or NHTSA)) THIS APPLIES TO ALL 84-88 4 cylinders. 87-88 was suposed to be better about it but don't trust that to be true.||||reg||1
